#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.154.0 — POS 2 REWRITE (voice correction pass 2 of 8
for the ZhiftPOS track).

The Point of Sale Profile in plain, trainee-neutral voice: the
groundwork records, items/prices/tax, all five tabs field by field,
the user table, and the Power Approvers. Replaces POS 2's lessons
AND question bank in place. TEN chapters.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_pos_pro.refresh_lessons --kwargs "{'only': 'pos_profile'}"
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_pos_pro.refresh_questions --kwargs "{'only': 'pos_profile'}"

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.153.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_pos_pro_data.json"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The profile: how to approach it", "10", "<p>This module walks through the Point of Sale Profile field by field. It is the longest module in the track because the profile is where every counter rule lives. Before the fields, three working principles.</p><p><b>1. Every setting is a decision.</b> Most fields on the profile have defaults, and a counter will run on the defaults. But a default is still a policy — it just is not one anyone chose. When you set up a profile, go through each consequential field and decide it on purpose. The rest of this module tells you what each field does and what depends on it, so those decisions are informed ones.</p><p><b>2. The right people decide, the administrator enters.</b> The administrator holds the pen, but many settings are really business decisions that belong to specific people. The discount mode is a decision about margin — whoever owns pricing should decide it. The cash refund switch is a decision about money leaving the drawer — whoever owns the money should decide it. The voucher settings belong with whoever runs the voucher programme. A good habit during setup: for each important field, note what was chosen, who chose it, and why. Later, when someone asks why the counter behaves a certain way, the answer is on record instead of in somebody's memory.</p><p><b>3. Changing a live profile is changing the rules.</b> A profile edit takes effect for every sale after it. Extending the return window for one insistent customer, lifting the override requirement for a busy season, enabling negative stock temporarily — each of these changes the counter's rules for everyone, and each is easy to make and easy to forget. Treat profile changes the way you treat the original setup: decided by the right person, noted with a reason, and reviewed periodically. A yearly walk through each profile, comparing the settings against the notes, catches the changes nobody remembers making.</p><p><b>How many profiles?</b> Module 1 gave the rule; here it is again because this module starts by applying it. Use one shared profile where every till in a shop should behave identically — one set of rules, one record to maintain, with terminals still reported individually. Create a separate profile only where a counter genuinely needs different rules — a returns desk with different permissions, a till with different hardware behaviour or a different float. The number of profiles should equal the number of genuinely different policies.</p><p><b>The module's route.</b> Chapter 2 covers the records that must exist before the profile can be created. Chapter 3 covers items, prices, tax and payment methods — the records every scan touches. Chapters 4 to 8 walk the profile itself: the Setup tab, the Pricing &amp; Discounts tab, the Payments &amp; Returns tab, the Gift Vouchers tab, and finally the Integrations tab together with the user table and the Power Approver table. Chapter 9 builds Okelewo's three profiles, and Chapter 10 reviews and closes with the module's law: <b>every counter power is a profile decision with a named owner.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Before the profile: ten records that must exist", "13", "<p>The profile points at records that must already exist in the back office. If they are right, creating the profile takes minutes. If they are wrong — the wrong company, a wrong warehouse — sales post incorrectly and correcting the postings afterwards can take days. Check this list, in this order, before creating any profile.</p><p><b>1. Company.</b> The legal entity that will own the sales. Confirm its default currency and chart of accounts. A profile pointed at the wrong company posts every sale into the wrong set of books.</p><p><b>2. Cost centre.</b> Where the shop's trading is recorded for reporting. The usual pattern is one cost centre per branch, which is what makes per-branch profit figures possible later.</p><p><b>3. Warehouse.</b> The stock location the till sells from. This must be a <b>leaf warehouse</b> — an actual location, not a group heading. The till can only sell what this warehouse holds, and the profile will not accept a group.</p><p><b>4. Branch.</b> The record that represents the shop itself. It ties the company, warehouse and cost centre together, and it is the record each terminal gets locked to during commissioning (module 3). Name branches clearly and distinctly — during commissioning someone will pick the branch from a list, and picking the wrong one sends sales to the wrong shop.</p><p><b>5. Price list.</b> A selling price list in the company currency, with rates loaded for the items the counter will sell. Chapter 3 covers the rules about rates and their validity dates.</p><p><b>6. Sales tax template.</b> The profile will not save without one. Tax on every bill is calculated from this template.</p><p><b>7. Payment methods.</b> One record for each way money can arrive — cash, card, transfer, and so on. Chapter 3 covers the three method names with special behaviour.</p><p><b>8. Customer group and territory defaults.</b> Where customers created at the counter are filed. Deciding these up front means every cashier files new customers the same way.</p><p><b>9. A walk-in customer.</b> The single customer record that unnamed sales book to. Create one deliberately; do not let each cashier invent their own.</p><p><b>10. User accounts.</b> One account per cashier, each with a working email address and a password the person knows. The email matters: the one-time approval codes from module 1 are delivered to approver emails, and password resets need them too.</p><p><b>The final check: sell one item from the back office.</b> Before commissioning any terminal, complete one sale end to end from the back office itself, using the same warehouse and tax template the profile will use. If that sale posts cleanly, the foundation is sound — the till is a fast front end onto the same posting machinery, so a clean back-office sale means the item, price, tax, warehouse and accounts are all correctly wired. If it fails, you have found the problem before a terminal added its own variables. Do this once per branch and keep the test invoice number in your setup notes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Items, prices, tax and payment methods", "13", "<p>Every scan at the till touches an item record, a price, a tax template and eventually a payment method. This chapter covers the rules for each — including three that cause most of the pricing problems seen in practice.</p><p><b>Items.</b> The fields that matter at the counter:</p><p>• <b>Barcode</b>, if the shop scans, and a reasonably short item name — long names are cut off in the cart display.<br>• <b>Taxable flag.</b> Tick it for standard-rated goods, leave it off for exempt ones. The till honours it per line, so a mixed basket taxes correctly.<br>• <b>Batch and serial settings.</b> An item that should be tracked by batch or serial must be created that way on the item record. If it is not, the till has no way to ask which batch or serial is being sold.<br>• <b>Non-stock items</b> are supported for services and fees — they skip stock checks.<br>• <b>Kiosk flags</b> mark which items appear when a terminal runs in kiosk mode.</p><p><b>Prices — and the zero-rate trap.</b> Rates are loaded against the profile's selling price list. Two rules to memorise: <b>1)</b> An item with no valid rate scans at <b>zero</b>. The till flags it but does not block the sale — so an unpriced item can be sold for nothing while everyone assumes it had a price. <b>2)</b> A rate outside its validity window counts as no rate at all. A rate whose start date has not arrived, or whose end date has passed, is ignored — this is the usual cause when items suddenly ring up at nil. When you get a pricing complaint, check the rate's validity dates first. Also note: where a batch carries its own price, the batch price is used ahead of the list rate. Price changes reach terminals on the next price refresh — no restart is needed.</p><p><b>Tax — one template per treatment.</b> Tax is calculated from the profile's tax template at the moment the bill is created. Two settings interact: the per-item Taxable flag, and the profile's <b>Is Tax Inclusive</b> switch (whether shelf prices already contain tax). Here is the trap: switching Is Tax Inclusive <b>rewrites the tax template itself</b>, marking its lines as included in the printed price or not. So if two profiles with opposite tax treatments point at the <b>same</b> template, each save rewrites the other's behaviour, and both counters tax wrongly in turn. The rule: <b>give each tax treatment its own template.</b> An inclusive-priced counter and an exclusive-priced counter must never share one.</p><p><b>Payment methods.</b> Every tender is a payment method record added to the profile's payments table. The order of rows in that table is the order cashiers see on the payment screen — put the most-used method first. Three method names have special behaviour built in:</p><p>• <b>Cash</b> prompts for the amount tendered, calculates change, opens the drawer, and works offline.<br>• <b>Store Credit</b> draws down the customer's stored balance. The balance lives on the server, so store credit is <b>unavailable offline</b> — a balance the till cannot verify is a balance it will not spend.<br>• <b>Gift Voucher</b> appears automatically when vouchers are enabled on the profile.</p><p>Card, transfer and similar methods simply record the amount — no live call is made, so they work offline. The exception is an integrated card terminal, covered on the Integrations tab in Chapter 8.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Tab 1: Setup", "14", "<p>The Setup tab holds the counter's day-to-day operating settings: customer defaults, cart behaviour, stock behaviour, shift management, and terminal switches. Field by field:</p><p><b>Customer defaults.</b></p><p>• <b>Default customer group / territory</b> — where counter-created customers are filed (decided in Chapter 2).<br>• <b>Allow Duplicate Customer Names</b> — when off, the till refuses to create a second customer with a name that already exists. Leave it off unless genuinely common names cause repeated blocks; if you turn it on, rely on the monthly duplicate scan (module 6) to merge the results.<br>• <b>Max Items in Search Results</b> — caps the product grid so search stays fast on modest hardware.</p><p><b>Cart rules.</b></p><p>• <b>Allow Clear Cart</b> — whether a cashier can discard a basket. Note the interplay with approvers: when this is <b>off</b> but a Power Approver holds the Clear Cart permission, the Clear button still appears but requires an approval. That makes discarding a basket a recorded, authorised event rather than an impossible one — which matters, because building a basket, taking cash, and then clearing instead of completing is a known fraud pattern (module 8 covers how to check for it).<br>• <b>Clear Cart On Logout</b> — whether an unfinished basket survives a sign-out. Turn it on where an abandoned basket must never carry over to the next cashier.<br>• <b>Allow Checkout Item Update</b> — whether quantities can still be edited on the payment screen.<br>• <b>Enable Item Queue Options</b> — adds a toggle at the till between <b>stack</b> mode (re-scanning an item increases its existing line) and <b>queue</b> mode (each scan makes its own line — useful where each unit needs its own serial or batch).<br>• <b>Delete Cart Queue / Delete After (days)</b> — automatic housekeeping for held baskets: parked carts older than the set age are removed.</p><p><b>Inventory and stock.</b></p><p>• <b>Allow Viewing Other Warehouses</b> — lets the counter answer is-it-at-the-other-branch questions from the Inventory Ops screen.<br>• <b>Allow Negative Stock</b> — permits selling below recorded stock. This setting <b>mirrors a company-wide stock setting and will not save unless that setting also allows it</b>. The profile cannot quietly permit what the company's inventory policy forbids, so treat a request to enable it as an inventory-policy question, not a till setting.<br>• <b>Enable Auto Allocate Batch</b> — the till picks batches automatically, <b>oldest first</b>, and only asks the cashier when the quantity cannot be covered. Turn it on for goods with expiry dates (oldest-first keeps stock rotating); leave it off where the cashier should choose, for example serialised or mixed-provenance goods.</p><p><b>Shift, terminal and access.</b></p><p>• <b>Enable Shift Management</b> — requires a shift to be opened before selling and closed with a counted drawer. Any counter that handles cash should have this on; module 4 covers the routine.<br>• <b>Enable Offline Login</b> — lets a cashier sign in against the terminal's stored record when the server is unreachable. It requires a prior successful online sign-in on that terminal, and expires <b>seven days</b> after the last one. Turn it on wherever power or connectivity is unreliable.<br>• <b>Enable Kiosk Mode</b> — the self-service display of kiosk-flagged items.<br>• <b>Enable Messages</b> — the Message Hub on this counter. Turn it off at counters that should not handle transfer requests.<br>• <b>Allow Transaction Date Edit</b> — permits back-dating a sale (never future-dating). Grant this sparingly: a back-dated sale moves revenue from one reporting day to another.<br>• <b>Enable Airtime Sales</b> — the Airtime console (module 6), switched per counter.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Tab 2: Pricing & Discounts", "14", "<p>The Pricing &amp; Discounts tab decides how a cashier can reduce a price. Its first field is the most consequential setting on the whole profile.</p><p><b>Discount Mode.</b> Three options, each a different way of controlling discounts:</p><p>• <b>Standard.</b> The cashier types a discount directly on the line. If the profile requires an override, an approver clears it at that moment. This suits a small shop with a supervisor on the floor — it is fast, and exactly as controlled as the supervisor's presence.<br>• <b>Approval-based.</b> Every discount must carry a <b>reason</b> chosen from the markdown reason list plus a typed <b>explanation</b> — neither can be blank. The reason is stored on the sale line and appears in markdown reporting, so at month end you can see not just how much was discounted but <b>why</b>. This is the recommended default for most businesses. Module 5 covers the markdown reasons themselves.<br>• <b>Code-based.</b> The cashier cannot type a discount at all. They raise a request from the cart; a category lead reviews it in the back office and approves (possibly changing the amount) or rejects; an approved request returns a single-use code the cashier applies. Every stage is timestamped. This gives the strongest control at the cost of a round trip, so it suits high-value negotiated goods rather than fast-moving counters. Module 5 walks the full cycle.</p><p>Choose the mode deliberately, based on the goods and the floor — do not leave it on its default.</p><p><b>Override Mode (password or one-time code).</b> Where an approval is required, it can be given two ways, and the choice is a field here:</p><p>• <b>Password</b> — the approver enters their email and password at the till. Fast, and works offline, but the approver must be physically present.<br>• <b>One-Time Code</b> — the till requests a six-digit code, which is emailed to <b>every</b> approver holding the relevant permission; any of them can read it to the cashier by phone. Codes expire after one hour, and the terminal must be online to request one.</p><p>Pick password where a supervisor is resident; pick codes where approvers are often away. Note a useful side effect of the code design: because every code request is emailed to all holders of that permission, no discount can be quietly arranged with just one approver — the whole group sees every request.</p><p><b>The remaining fields.</b></p><p>• <b>Allow Discount Edit</b> — the master switch. Off, the discount control is disabled on every line regardless of mode. A counter that simply does not discount is one unticked box.<br>• <b>Allow Rate Edit</b> and <b>Require Override for Rate Edit</b> — rate editing lets a cashier overwrite the unit price itself, which is a stronger power than discounting (a discount is limited by the price; a rate edit replaces it). Edited lines are marked <b>Rate override</b> so they are visible on review. If you allow rate editing at all, turn the override requirement on as well.<br>• <b>Enable Promotions</b> / <b>Enable Promotion Code</b> — switches on the campaign machinery (module 5). One behaviour to know now: a line that has received a promotional discount is locked — no further manual discount or rate change can be added on top of it.<br>• <b>Is Tax Inclusive</b> — covered in Chapter 3; remember the shared-template rule.<br>• <b>Allow Shipping Cost</b> — adds an editable delivery fee line to the cart.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Tab 3: Payments & Returns", "14", "<p>The Payments &amp; Returns tab governs credit, refunds, lay-by and staff purchases — every way money can leave later, leave backwards, or leave discounted.</p><p><b>Customer and payment settings.</b></p><p>• <b>Allow Partial Payment</b> — a sale can complete with less than full payment, the balance owing on the customer's account. This is the counter extending credit; only enable it where customer accounts are actually managed.<br>• <b>Enable Credit Limit Check</b> — shows the customer's balance and limit on the cart header, and <b>suspends selling</b> when the limit is breached (the cart shows: Sales suspended due to customer outstanding). Only a Power Approver holding the <b>Credit Limit Override</b> permission can release the sale. The release is a recorded approval.<br>• <b>Allow Customer Credit Against Invoice</b> — carries an unpaid balance on the invoice itself.<br>• <b>Allow Loyalty Points</b> — earning and redemption at this counter. Redemption is calculated on the server, so it is unavailable offline.</p><p><b>Returns.</b> Four settings that work together:</p><p>• <b>Allow Cash Refund</b> — whether money can leave the drawer on a return. When <b>off</b>, the only refund route is <b>store credit</b>. Starting a new deployment with cash refund off is a sound default: store credit keeps the refund inside the system, while cash refunds open the drawer to the phantom-return pattern from module 1. Enable cash refunds later, deliberately, with the override requirement on.<br>• <b>Allow Return Exchange</b> — goods swapped for goods, with the difference settled in either direction.<br>• <b>Require Override for Returns</b> + <b>Return Override Mode</b> — every return needs an approver, by password or code as configured.<br>• <b>Return Policy In Days</b> (default thirty) — the return window. A return <b>beyond</b> the window is still possible, but requires an override — the window is a rule with a supervised exception, not a hard wall.</p><p>One visibility rule worth memorising: the <b>Return &amp; Exchange console only appears in the sidebar when at least one of cash refund, exchange, or the return policy is enabled</b>. If none is enabled, there is no console at all — remember this when a returns menu seems to be missing.</p><p><b>Lay-By.</b> Three numbers configure it:</p><p>• <b>Lay-By Deposit %</b> — the minimum taken when the lay-by is created (default ten).<br>• <b>Lay-By Installments</b> — how many payments the balance splits into (default three).<br>• <b>Lay-By Day Spacing</b> — comma-separated days from booking when instalments fall due; 0, 30, 90 means at once, after a month, after three months.</p><p>If the number of spacing values and the instalment count disagree, <b>the count wins</b> and the schedule is filled from the values available — but treat a mismatch as a mistake to fix, not behaviour to rely on. Provide exactly as many spacings as instalments.</p><p><b>Staff Purchase.</b> Two fields here, plus dependencies elsewhere:</p><p>• <b>Enable Staff Purchase</b> — switches the console on for this counter.<br>• <b>Staff Purchase Discount %</b> — applied automatically to every line. If both this and a company-wide staff discount exist, <b>the profile's percentage takes precedence</b> at this counter.</p><p>For the feature to work end to end, three more things must exist: a <b>purchase limit</b> on each employee's record, and in the company-wide settings, the <b>employee purchase account</b> (where balances sit) and the <b>salary component</b> used to recover them through payroll. Module 6 covers the flow; set the wiring up here.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Tab 4: Gift Vouchers", "13", "<p>A gift voucher is a bearer instrument: whoever holds the code can spend it, exactly like cash. Because of that, everything on this tab is deliberately conservative — features are <b>off until you turn them on</b>, and each one you enable should be a considered decision. Module 7 covers running the voucher programme; this chapter covers its settings.</p><p><b>The core settings.</b></p><p>• <b>Enable Gift Voucher</b> — the master switch. It adds the voucher tile to the Sales Console and Gift Voucher as a payment method.<br>• <b>Voucher Denominations</b> — comma-separated face values shown as quick buttons when selling a voucher. Cashiers can still type another amount; the presets just speed up the common cases.<br>• <b>Enable Voucher Partial Redemption</b> — a voucher worth more than the bill keeps its remaining balance instead of being used up in one go.<br>• <b>Voucher Change Policy</b> — what happens to any surplus when a voucher exceeds the bill: <b>Remainder as Balance</b> keeps it on the voucher; <b>No Change</b> forfeits it. Decide this once, and make sure the printed terms match — customers will hold you to whichever was printed.</p><p><b>The offline policy — read this one carefully.</b> A voucher's balance lives on the server, and a terminal that is offline cannot verify it. The <b>Voucher Offline Policy</b> field decides what happens then:</p><p>• <b>Online Only</b> (the default) — all voucher activity is refused while the terminal is offline, and the cashier is told so plainly. This is the safe choice: no voucher can be spent twice during an outage because none can be spent at all.<br>• <b>Guarded Offline</b> — voucher activity is permitted offline, within two limits you set: <b>Offline Max Value</b> (the largest single voucher redeemable offline) and <b>Offline Daily Cap</b> (the total voucher value one terminal may redeem offline per day). These caps are the business's maximum exposure if the same voucher is spent at two terminals during an outage. Set them by asking what the business can afford to lose on a bad day — and revisit them as voucher volumes grow.</p><p><b>The extended switches.</b> Each off by default; enable only what the voucher programme actually needs:</p><p>• <b>Enable Voucher Top-up</b> — value can be added to an existing voucher.<br>• <b>Enable Voucher Expiry</b> — vouchers carry an expiry date and stop working after it. This is what makes lapsed-value accounting possible (module 7).<br>• <b>Enable Corporate Vouchers</b> — bulk voucher issues to a named corporate customer.<br>• <b>Enable Refund to Voucher</b> — a gift voucher can be a refund destination on returns, similar in spirit to store credit.<br>• <b>Allow Voucher Buy-back</b> — the shop redeems an unspent voucher for <b>cash</b>. This is the most dangerous switch on the tab, because it converts a bearer instrument directly into money out of the drawer. Leave it off unless there is a firm business reason, and if it must be on, require an override and watch its usage (module 8 explains what to watch).<br>• <b>Enforce Voucher Booking</b> — a voucher is reserved centrally before it can be issued at a counter. This closes the small window in which two tills could otherwise issue the <b>same</b> code at the same time. Any shop with more than one till should turn it on.</p><p><b>One rule to carry forward.</b> Unless Guarded Offline was deliberately chosen, any sale that sells or redeems a voucher is refused while offline — and the till tells the cashier why. That refusal is the design working, not a fault.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Tab 5, the user table, and the Power Approvers", "14", "<p>The last tab, and the two tables that control who can do what.</p><p><b>Tab 5: Integrations.</b> This tab wires an integrated card terminal to the till, so a card payment is pushed to the device and the till waits for the result (instead of the cashier keying the amount into a standalone machine).</p><p>• <b>Enable Payment Gateway</b> — the master switch.<br>• <b>Live Environment</b> — whether the connection points at the provider's live system or their test one. Follow this sequence exactly: commission with Live Environment <b>off</b>, process a successful test transaction, and only then switch it on. Note the switch date both times.<br>• <b>Credentials</b> — merchant identifier, public key, private key. The private key is stored masked. Treat these like passwords: store them properly, and rotate them if staff with access leave.<br>• <b>Terminals table</b> — one row per physical card machine, so the till knows which device receives a payment.</p><p>Offline behaviour: an integrated card payment needs a live connection. When offline, use cash, a manually keyed card entry, or transfer.</p><p><b>The user table.</b> One row per person who signs in at this counter. Each row carries the person's account and a set of <b>access boxes</b> that decide which consoles they see:</p><p>• <b>Standard</b> — the Sales Console (ordinary selling).<br>• <b>Order</b> and <b>Checkout</b> — the two stages of Pharmacy mode. Note: the Checkout box also grants <b>Revenue Intelligence</b> access, since the person handling payment is the one who needs the money reports.<br>• <b>Booking</b> and <b>Cashier</b> — the two stages of Booking mode.<br>• <b>Inventory Ops</b> — stock enquiry, material requests and transfers. On by default.</p><p>Three mechanics to know: <b>1)</b> The boxes are <b>normalised against the profile's selling mode when the profile is saved</b> — Standard mode grants everyone Standard and clears the two-stage boxes; Pharmacy clears the booking boxes. This is why module 1 said to set the mode first. <b>2)</b> One row per user is marked as their <b>default profile</b> — the one they sign into automatically. <b>3)</b> On sign-in, the till opens on the first console the person is entitled to: Sales in Standard mode, Checkout or Order in Pharmacy, Cashier or Booking in Booking mode; someone with only Inventory Ops lands there. Useful for support: where someone lands tells you what their row grants.</p><p><b>The Power Approver table.</b> One row per approver, ticking only the permissions that person should hold: <b>Discount, Returns</b> (including beyond-window returns), <b>Clear Cart, Rate,</b> and <b>Credit Limit Override</b>. Grant each box deliberately — the boxes are independent, and module 1's Okelewo decision (Returns not bundled with Discount) is the pattern to copy.</p><p><b>The no-approver trap.</b> If the profile requires an override for some action but <b>no approver holds that permission</b>, the control is simply <b>disabled</b>, and the cashier is told no approvers are configured. So: always configure at least one approver for every permission the profile requires — and before go-live, <b>test every override path with a real approval</b>. A discount that cannot be approved at six in the evening on opening day is a queue of customers and an emergency nobody needs.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: building Okelewo's profiles", "15", "<p>One afternoon of setup, using everything in this module. Okelewo builds three profiles — one per branch, since each branch's tills follow one policy.</p><p><b>The groundwork check (Chapters 2-3).</b> The ten-record checklist runs first and finds three problems — which is normal, and is exactly why the checklist runs first:</p><p>• The Lagos shop had no leaf warehouse — a group warehouse had been standing in. A proper leaf warehouse is created before anything else.<br>• The price list contains about forty items whose rate validity windows have <b>expired</b> — each of these would have scanned at zero. The windows are fixed, and a quarterly price list review is added to the calendar so it does not recur.<br>• The draft plan pointed both the flagship (tax-inclusive shelf prices) and the Lagos shop (tax-exclusive) at <b>one</b> tax template. Chapter 3's rule applies: each treatment gets its own template, split before the first profile is saved.</p><p>The back-office test sale then posts cleanly at each branch. The payments table is ordered by actual frequency at the counter: cash, transfer, card. All eleven cashier accounts exist with working emails.</p><p><b>Tab 1 decisions (Chapter 4).</b> Shift management <b>on</b> at all branches — the counted drawer is the point of the whole rollout. Offline login <b>on</b> — power in Abeokuta makes it necessary. Auto-allocate batch <b>on</b> at the flagship's provisions counter (expiry-dated goods, oldest first) and <b>off</b> at the Lagos electronics section (serials — the cashier should choose). Transaction date edit <b>off</b> for everyone. Duplicate customer names refused, with the monthly merge scan noted for module 6.</p><p><b>Tab 2 decisions (Chapter 5).</b> Discount mode: <b>Approval-based</b> at all three branches — the MD wants to know why margin is given away, not just how much. Five markdown reasons are drafted (damaged, price match, display item, expiring stock, goodwill) for module 5. Override mode: <b>password</b> at the two Abeokuta shops, where supervisors are resident; <b>one-time codes</b> at Lagos, where the approver travels — accepting that code requests need the terminal online.</p><p><b>Tab 3 decisions (Chapter 6).</b> Cash refund <b>off</b> at launch — refunds go to store credit only, to be revisited after ninety days of returns data. Returns override <b>required</b>, window thirty days. Lay-By: deposit raised to <b>15%</b> after the MD weighs the risk of abandoned reservations, three instalments, spacing 0, 30, 60. Staff purchase enabled at the flagship only, with the employee purchase account and salary component confirmed in company settings before the switch is ticked.</p><p><b>Tab 4 (Chapter 7).</b> Left dark for now, deliberately: the master switch stays off until the voucher accounts exist (the accountant's condition — module 7 opens with it). The launch design is agreed in advance: Online Only, booking enforcement on for the multi-till flagship, buy-back permanently off.</p><p><b>Tab 5, users and approvers (Chapter 8).</b> No integrated card terminals yet, so the Integrations tab stays empty — noted as a decision, not an oversight. The user tables are filled after the mode is set (Standard everywhere for now). The approver table is built to module 1's casting: four approvers, boxes ticked per person, Returns not bundled with Discount, Credit Limit Override held by only two people. Then the go-live test from Chapter 8 catches a real one: the second Abeokuta shop's profile requires return overrides but has <b>no approver row</b> — on the test till, the return control shows disabled with the no-approvers message, exactly as the chapter warned. The row is added, and test every override path joins the permanent go-live checklist.</p><p><b>First sign-ins.</b> Each cashier signs in; landing consoles and sidebars match what the rows grant. The MD's note at the end of the afternoon: <i>we argued about checkboxes for four hours and I realised we were writing the shop's rules. Good. They were overdue.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Review & the second law", "12", "<p>Check yourself against each section. Everything later in the track assumes this module.</p><p><b>Approach (Chapter 1).</b> Every consequential field is decided on purpose, by the person who owns that policy, with the choice and reason noted. Profile changes on a live counter are rule changes — noted and reviewed. Profile count = policy count.</p><p><b>Groundwork (Chapter 2).</b> Ten records before any profile: company, cost centre, leaf warehouse, branch (named unmistakably), price list, tax template, payment methods, customer group and territory defaults, one walk-in customer, and individual user accounts with working emails. Then one test sale from the back office — if it posts cleanly, the foundation is sound.</p><p><b>Items, prices, tax, tenders (Chapter 3).</b> The taxable flag works per line; batch and serial tracking must be set when the item is created. An item with no valid rate scans at zero, and a rate outside its validity window is no rate at all — check validity dates first on any pricing complaint. Is Tax Inclusive rewrites the shared template, so each tax treatment gets its own template. The payments table's row order is the display order. Cash calculates change and works offline; Store Credit needs the server; Gift Voucher appears when vouchers are enabled.</p><p><b>Setup tab (Chapter 4).</b> Clear Cart interacts with the approver table (off plus an approver = approval-required clearing). Stack versus queue mode. Held baskets age out. Negative stock mirrors the company setting. Auto-allocate picks oldest first. Shift management on for any cash counter. Offline login expires seven days after the last online sign-in. Transaction date edit moves revenue between reporting days — grant sparingly.</p><p><b>Pricing tab (Chapter 5).</b> Discount Mode is the most consequential field: Standard (direct), Approval-based (reason plus explanation, stored on the line — the recommended default), Code-based (request, central approval, single-use code). Password approvals are fast and offline but need presence; one-time codes work remotely, email every holder, expire in an hour. Rate editing outranks discounting — mark it and require overrides. Promotional discounts lock their line.</p><p><b>Payments &amp; Returns tab (Chapter 6).</b> Credit limit breaches suspend the sale until a Credit Limit Override approver releases it. Cash refund off means store-credit-only — the sound launch default. Returns beyond the window need an override. The Return &amp; Exchange console only appears when at least one returns feature is enabled. Lay-by: deposit %, instalments, day spacing — count wins on a mismatch. Staff purchase needs the employee limit, the purchase account and the salary component wired.</p><p><b>Voucher tab (Chapter 7).</b> Everything off until deliberately on. Online Only is the safe offline policy; Guarded Offline is a chosen exposure with two caps. Booking enforcement for any multi-till shop. Buy-back converts vouchers to drawer cash — the most dangerous switch.</p><p><b>Integrations, users, approvers (Chapter 8).</b> Live Environment: test first, then switch. Access boxes normalise to the mode on save; Checkout carries Revenue Intelligence; the landing console reveals the row. Approver permissions are five independent boxes. No approver for a required permission = a disabled control — configure at least one, and test every override path before go-live.</p><p><b>The second law: every counter power is a profile decision with a named owner.</b> Module 3 takes the finished profiles to the hardware: installing, licensing and commissioning the terminals.</p>"),
]

